Track 2: "Why Answer The Sea Smoke?"
- Story & Context: Drifting off the fog-choked Atlantic shelf near Takoradi, third-mate and synthesist Ama Serwaa locked herself inside the wheelhouse of the freighter MV Volta Queen while the radar screens flooded with false blinding halos of freezing sea smoke. Sifting through the captain’s forbidden cassette deck, she patched an automated Roland Juno-106 bassline through the ship’s primary collision horn, desperate to hear if any passing vessel would reciprocate her rhythmic confession. The reply did not come from an officer on watch, but from an approaching Liberian tanker whose deckhands slammed an empty steel shipping container with sledgehammers to complete her syncopated highlife groove. Weeping as salt-crust dried on her cold wool collar, Serwaa sang into the open maritime distress channel: "I gave my coordinates to the blindness, but nobody steers with both hands."
